'''Astrology and astronomy''' were archaically treated together ({{langx|la|astrologia}}), but gradually distinguished through the [[Late Middle Ages]]<ref>{{cite book|last=Pedersen|first=Olaf|title=Early physics and astronomy : a historical introduction|year=1993|publisher=[[Cambridge University Press]]|location=Cambridge [England]|isbn=978-0521403405|pages=214|chapter=Chapter Eighteen: Medieval Astronomy|edition=Rev.}}</ref> into the [[Age of Enlightenment|Age of Reason]]. Developments in [[17th century philosophy]] resulted in [[astrology]] and [[astronomy]] operating as independent pursuits by the 18th century.

Whereas the [[academic discipline]] of astronomy studies observable [[phenomenon|phenomena]] beyond the Earth's atmosphere,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://concise.britannica.com/ebc/article-9356013/astronomy |title=astronomy – Britannica Concise |publisher=Concise.britannica.com |access-date=20 November 2011 |archive-date=3 February 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080203063907/http://concise.britannica.com/ebc/article-9356013/astronomy |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ontariosciencecentre.ca/school/clc/visits/glossary.asp |title=Ontario Science Centre: Glossary of Useful Scientific Terms |publisher=Ontariosciencecentre.ca |access-date=20 November 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://library.thinkquest.org/5014/glossary.html |title=Outer Space Glossary |publisher=Library.thinkquest.org |access-date=20 November 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111007224627/http://library.thinkquest.org/5014/glossary.html |archive-date=7 October 2011 }}</ref> astrology uses the apparent positions of celestial objects as the basis for [[divination]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://concise.britannica.com/ebc/article-9356010/astrology |title=astrology – Britannica Concise |publisher=Concise.britannica.com |access-date=20 November 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite web
