{{History of Greek art}}

'''Greek art''' began in the [[Cycladic civilization|Cycladic]], [[Minoan civilization|Minoan]] and [[Mycenaean civilization]], and gave birth to [[Classicism|Western classical art]] in the subsequent [[Geometric art|Geometric]], [[Archaic Greece|Archaic]] and [[Classical Greece|Classical]] periods (with further developments during the [[Hellenistic]] Period). It absorbed influences of Eastern civilizations, of Roman art and its patrons, and the new religion of [[Eastern Orthodox Church|Orthodox Christianity]] in the [[Byzantine Empire|Byzantine]] era and absorbed [[Italy|Italian]] and [[Europe]]an ideas during the period of [[Romanticism]] (with the invigoration of the [[Greek Revolution]]), until the [[Modernist]] and [[Postmodernist]].
Greek art is mainly five forms: [[Ancient Greek architecture|architecture]], [[Ancient Greek sculpture|sculpture]], [[painting]], [[Pottery of ancient Greece|pottery]] and [[Jewellery|jewelry making]].

